3.1 Gradient Descent
At this point we are over half way through the module and we have not yet discussed the mechanics of how a machine learning model is actually trained. That is, while we have defined optimisation objectives that we might seek to minimise, we have not discussed how such minimisation is performed.
As we have already acknowledged in Section 2.2.3.2, optimisation over a high-dimensional parameter space \Theta is usually extremely difficult. A further complication is that the empirical risk \mathcal{R}_n can be expensive to calculate; we have to sum across the full dataset of size n, and for modern machine learning tasks with enormous datasets even computing this sum can be a challenging computational task.
The dominant solution in machine learning is stochastic optimisation, and we are going to study these methods in detail. At a high level, at least, we can view stochastic optimisation as a fast but approximate numerical method for global optimisation; it may not find global optima, but it often finds the “shallow” local minima that correspond to machine learning models which generalise well.
The simplest example of stochastic optimisation is called stochastic gradient descent, and we study this in Section 3.2. As a warm-up, we first consider the analysis of regular gradient descent in Section 3.1.
Some practically important extensions of stochastic gradient descent are highlighted in Section 3.3.
Computational frameworks that implement stochastic optimisation build on automatic differentiation, and such is its importance in enabling modern machine learning we devote Section 3.4 to this topic.
For the purposes of this section we are going to focus on empirical risk minimisation (cf. Definition 1.4), which we recall seeks parameters \hat{\bm{\theta}} for which the empirical risk 
\mathcal{R}_n(f_{\bm{\theta}}) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n L(\mathbf{y}_i , f_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_i) )
 is minimised; an extension to include additive regularisation (cf. Definition 2.2) is straightforward.
A simple heuristic is to initialise at some parameter values \bm{\theta}_0 and iteratively “follow the gradient downhill”, constructing a sequence (\bm{\theta}_t)_{t \in \mathbb{N}} that moves toward a local minimum of the empirical risk: 
\bm{\theta}_{t+1} = \bm{\theta}_t - \epsilon \nabla_{\bm{\theta}} \mathcal{R}_n(f_{\bm{\theta}}) |_{\bm{\theta} = \bm{\theta}_t}.
\tag{3.1} Here \epsilon &gt; 0 is called a learning rate and should be selected carefully so that the sequence (\bm{\theta}_t)_{t \in \mathbb{N}} “settles nicely” into a local minimum of the empirical risk (we will discuss the choice of \epsilon theoretically in Theorem 3.2 and practically in ). In practice Equation 3.1 is terminiated after a finite number of steps T have been performed, and the resulting \bm{\theta}_T is viewed as an approximation to \hat{\bm{\theta}}.
The gradient of the empirical risk follows from the chain rule, as 
\nabla_{\bm{\theta}} \mathcal{R}_n(f_{\bm{\theta}}) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \nabla_2 L( \mathbf{y}_i , f_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_i) ) \nabla_{\bm{\theta}} f_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_i)  
\tag{3.2} where \nabla_2 L denotes the gradient of L with respect to its second argument.
Calculating \nabla_2 L is usually straightforward; for example for the squared error loss L(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{z}) = \|\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{z}\|^2 we have \nabla_2 L(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{z}) = - 2 (\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{z}).
Calculating \nabla_{\bm{\theta}} f_{\bm{\theta}} is in principle more difficult, but huge progress has been made in this direction through the emergence of automatic differentiation frameworks such as Torch and JAX (cf. Section 3.4), to the extent that differentiating through the machine learning model is often practically straightforward.
General pseudocode for gradient descent is presented in Algorithm 1. To reinforce ideas, let’s perform a simple example of gradient descent:
Gradient descent is an intuitive idea, but does it work in general?
To take a first step toward analysing gradient descent, we consider the favourable case of optimising a convex objective function:
An example of a convex function is shown in Figure 3.1.
The differentiable convex functions are special; they enable local information (i.e. a vanishing gradient \nabla F(\bm{\theta}_\star) = \bm{0}) to be used to deduce global optimality (i.e. F(\bm{\theta}_\star) = \min_{\bm{\theta} \in \mathbb{R}^p} F(\bm{\theta})).
{
  const width = 500;
  const height = 320;
  const margin = { top: 20, right: 30, bottom: 50, left: 50 };

  const svg = d3.create("svg")
    .attr("viewBox", [0, 0, width, height])
    .attr("width", width)
    .attr("height", height);

  // Scales
  const xMin = -1.0, xMax = 4.0;
  const yMin = 0, yMax = 3.6;

  const xScale = d3.scaleLinear()
    .domain([xMin, xMax])
    .range([margin.left, width - margin.right]);

  const yScale = d3.scaleLinear()
    .domain([yMin, yMax])
    .range([height - margin.bottom, margin.top]);

  // Function F(x) = 1 + 0.25*(x-1.2)^2 + 0.8*exp(-0.8*(x+0.2))
  function F(x) {
    return 1 + 0.25 * Math.pow(x - 1.2, 2) + 0.8 * Math.exp(-0.8 * (x + 0.2));
  }

  // Derivative F'(x) = 0.5*(x-1.2) - 0.64*exp(-0.8*(x+0.2))
  function Fprime(x) {
    return 0.5 * (x - 1.2) - 0.64 * Math.exp(-0.8 * (x + 0.2));
  }

  // Point of tangency
  const theta = 0.6;
  const Ftheta = F(theta);
  const slope = Fprime(theta);

  // Tangent line: y = F(theta) + F'(theta)*(x - theta)
  function tangent(x) {
    return Ftheta + slope * (x - theta);
  }

  // Draw axes
  // x-axis
  svg.append("line")
    .attr("x1", xScale(xMin)).attr("y1", yScale(0))
    .attr("x2", xScale(xMax) + 10).attr("y2", yScale(0))
    .attr("stroke", "black").attr("stroke-width", 1.5)
    .attr("marker-end", "url(#arrowhead)");

  // y-axis
  svg.append("line")
    .attr("x1", xScale(xMin)).attr("y1", yScale(0))
    .attr("x2", xScale(xMin)).attr("y2", yScale(yMax) - 10)
    .attr("stroke", "black").attr("stroke-width", 1.5)
    .attr("marker-end", "url(#arrowhead)");

  // Arrowhead marker
  svg.append("defs").append("marker")
    .attr("id", "arrowhead")
    .attr("viewBox", "0 -5 10 10")
    .attr("refX", 8).attr("refY", 0)
    .attr("markerWidth", 6).attr("markerHeight", 6)
    .attr("orient", "auto")
    .append("path")
    .attr("d", "M0,-5L10,0L0,5")
    .attr("fill", "black");

  // Axis label
  svg.append("text")
    .attr("x", xScale(xMax) + 15).attr("y", yScale(0) + 5)
    .attr("font-size", 14).attr("font-style", "italic")
    .text("ϑ");

  // Plot F(x)
  const line = d3.line()
    .x(d =&gt; xScale(d))
    .y(d =&gt; yScale(F(d)));

  const xValues = d3.range(xMin, xMax, 0.02);

  svg.append("path")
    .datum(xValues)
    .attr("fill", "none")
    .attr("stroke", "black")
    .attr("stroke-width", 2)
    .attr("d", line);

  // Label for F(ϑ)
  svg.append("text")
    .attr("x", xScale(2.1)).attr("y", yScale(2.4))
    .attr("font-size", 14).attr("font-style", "italic")
    .text("F(ϑ)");

  // Plot tangent line
  const tangentLine = d3.line()
    .x(d =&gt; xScale(d))
    .y(d =&gt; yScale(tangent(d)));

  const tangentXValues = d3.range(-0.5, 2.5, 0.1);

  svg.append("path")
    .datum(tangentXValues)
    .attr("fill", "none")
    .attr("stroke", "black")
    .attr("stroke-width", 1.5)
    .attr("d", tangentLine);

  // Label for tangent
  svg.append("text")
    .attr("x", xScale(2.6)).attr("y", yScale(0.5))
    .attr("font-size", 12).attr("font-style", "italic")
    .text("F(θ) + ∇F(θ)ᵀ(ϑ − θ)");

  // Mark tangency point
  svg.append("circle")
    .attr("cx", xScale(theta)).attr("cy", yScale(Ftheta))
    .attr("r", 4)
    .attr("fill", "black");

  // Dashed vertical line to axis
  svg.append("line")
    .attr("x1", xScale(theta)).attr("y1", yScale(Ftheta))
    .attr("x2", xScale(theta)).attr("y2", yScale(0))
    .attr("stroke", "black")
    .attr("stroke-width", 1)
    .attr("stroke-dasharray", "4,3");

  // Tick and label at theta
  svg.append("line")
    .attr("x1", xScale(theta)).attr("y1", yScale(0) - 4)
    .attr("x2", xScale(theta)).attr("y2", yScale(0) + 4)
    .attr("stroke", "black").attr("stroke-width", 1.5);

  svg.append("text")
    .attr("x", xScale(theta)).attr("y", yScale(0) + 20)
    .attr("text-anchor", "middle")
    .attr("font-size", 14).attr("font-style", "italic")
    .text("θ");

  return svg.node();
}








Figure 3.1: Illustrating a differentiable convex function F \colon \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}.
Since \bm{\theta}_\star is a minimiser of F, the left hand side of Equation 3.3 is \geq 0. The quantity 
\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} F(\bm{\theta}_t) - F(\bm{\theta}_\star)
\tag{3.4} is called the regret; it measures how much we suffered along the optimisation path, compared to if we had simply known \bm{\theta}_\star at the start (we will consider the concept of regret in more detail in Section 5.1).
Thus Theorem 3.1 shows that, if the learning rate \epsilon is small enough, and we are planning to take a large enough number of iterations T, then our regret will be small.
From Theorem 3.1 we can elicit a suitable choice of learning rate \epsilon by minimising the overall bound, leading to (cf. Question 7) 
\epsilon = \frac{\|\theta_0 - \theta_\star\|}{\sqrt{T} B} .
\tag{3.7} That is, the learning rate \epsilon should be smaller when either (a) the initial state \theta_0 is close to the minimiser \theta_\star, (b) the number of steps T to be taken is larger, or (c) we have a larger bound B on the stochastic gradient.
A strategy that is sometimes used in theoretical analysis of gradient descent is to make the learning rate time-dependent, i.e., \epsilon_t. For example, the choice \epsilon_t = O(t^{-1/2}) leads to the slightly stronger result 
\min_{0 \leq t \leq T-1} F(\theta_t) - F(\theta_\star) = O\left( \frac{\log(T)}{\sqrt{T}} \right) ,
 which you can see by re-running the proof of Theorem 3.1 with this choice of learning rate, or by taking a look at Bach (2024, Proposition 5.6).

3.2 Stochastic Gradient Descent
The main issue with gradient descent in machine learning is that every gradient step required computing the gradient of the empirical risk, which in turn requires summing over all n entries of the training dataset. It is common for dataset sizes to exceed n = 10^6, and a typical number of iterations needed for gradient descent might be T = 10^3, leading to a high overall computational cost O(nT).
To mitigate this computational cost, stochastic gradient descent replaces the full gradient with an approximation obtained from a randomly selected subset (often called a minibatch) of the training dataset: 
    \nabla_{\bm{\theta}} \mathcal{R}_n(f_{\bm{\theta}}) \approx \frac{1}{m} \sum_{j=1}^m \nabla_2 L(\mathbf{y}_{i_j} , f_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{i_j}) ) \nabla_{\bm{\theta}} f_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{i_j})
\tag{3.8} where \{i_j\}_{j=1}^m is a uniformly random subset2 of \{1,\dots,n\}.
The stochastic gradient Equation 3.8 is an unbiased estimator of the true gradient (cf. Question 6), and its variance is typically larger when the minibatch size m is small. Pseudocode for the general version of stochastic gradient descent is presented in Algorithm 2, and the idea is illustrated in Figure 3.2.

