\begin{algorithm} \caption{Stochastic Gradient Descent on $F : \mathbb{R}^p \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$} \begin{algorithmic} \Require stochastic gradients $G_t : \mathbb{R}^p \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^p$ with $\mathbb{E}[G_t(\cdot)] = \nabla F(\cdot)$ \Require initial state $\boldsymbol{\theta}_0 \in \mathbb{R}^p$ \Require learning rate $\epsilon \in (0,\infty)$ \Require number of steps $T$ \For{$t = 0, \dots, T-1$} \State $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t+1} \gets \boldsymbol{\theta}_t - \epsilon G_t(\boldsymbol{\theta}_t)$ \EndFor \end{algorithmic} \end{algorithm}


The critical design choices in stochastic gradient descent are thus the size m of the minibatches and the learning rate \epsilon. Theoretical analysis provides insight into how these degrees of freedom should be selected. The mathematical analysis of stochastic gradient descent applied to non-convex objectives is a rich topic, but for our purposes we will settle for the simplest possible analysis which directly generalises Theorem 3.1 to stochastic gradient descent:







Theorem 3.2 (Convergence of stochastic gradient descent) Let F : \mathbb{R}^p \rightarrow \mathbb{R} be convex and differentiable with \|\nabla F(\boldsymbol{\theta})\| \leq B for all \boldsymbol{\theta} \in \mathbb{R}^p. Let (G_t)_{t \geq 0} be independent and satisfy \mathbb{E}[G_t(\boldsymbol{\theta})] = \nabla F(\boldsymbol{\theta}) and \mathbb{E}[\|G_t(\boldsymbol{\theta})\|^2] \leq B^2 for all \boldsymbol{\theta} \in \mathbb{R}^p. Then the sequence (\boldsymbol{\theta}_t)_{t \geq 0} produced by stochastic gradient descent as in Algorithm 2 satisfies 
\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathbb{E}[F(\boldsymbol{\theta}_t)] - F(\boldsymbol{\theta}_\star) \leq \frac{\|\boldsymbol{\theta}_0 - \boldsymbol{\theta}_\star\|^2}{2\epsilon T} + \frac{\epsilon B^2}{2}
 where \boldsymbol{\theta}_\star denotes any minimiser of F.
